            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Financial Crisis and Reforms</strong><br>\nThe financial situation in France was dire when Louis XVI became king. France's involvement in the American Revolution had left the country deeply in debt. Louis appointed several financial advisors to reform the system, but their proposals often met with resistance. The Parlements (French law courts) opposed many of his suggested reforms, which included taxing the nobility.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Summoning the Estates-General</strong><br>\nWith France on the brink of financial ruin, Louis XVI summoned the Estates-General in 1789 for the first time in over 150 years. The Estates-General was an assembly representing France's clergy, nobility, and commoners. Louis hoped it would approve his financial reforms, but the meeting instead provided a platform for widespread discontent with the monarchy and the social structure of the time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The French Revolution Begins</strong><br>\nThe Estates-General quickly became a site of contention and eventually led to the formation of the National Assembly by the Third Estate, representing the common people. This assembly marked the beginning of the French Revolution. Louis XVI's attempts to control the situation failed, and his authority began to crumble as revolutionary ideas spread and the public grew increasingly hostile towards the monarchy.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
