          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Fall of the Monarchy</strong><br>\nAs revolutionary fervor escalated, Louis XVI's power was systematically stripped away. He attempted to flee France with his family in 1791 but was captured. This event undermined his credibility and the monarchy's position. In 1792, the monarchy was abolished by the newly formed National Convention, and France was declared a republic.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Trial and Execution</strong><br>\nAfter the monarchy was abolished, Louis XVI was tried by the National Convention and found guilty of conspiracy and treason against the state. His trial was highly political, reflecting the deep divisions and anger in French society. He was sentenced to death by guillotine and executed on January 21, 1793. His death marked a significant point in the history of France and the Revolution.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Louis XVI</strong><br>\nLouis XVI's legacy is complex. To some, he is a symbol of the excesses and failures of the monarchy, while to others, he is a tragic figure overcome by events beyond his control. His reign saw the end of the absolute monarchy in France and ushered in an era of profound social and political upheaval that would have lasting effects not only on France but on the history of Europe and the concept of governance.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
